\'ve been going through the moves. All the moves seem to have an impact.\n\nI need to minimize the number of pieces flipped to limit the opponent’s moves, especially in the opening stage. I think **C5** or **C6** are pretty good because they build pieces centrally and without over-flipping.\n\nHowever, playing **H4** is the key move. It secures a stable edge piece, and, critically, it defensively restricts a potentially strong opponent\'s move. By playing **H4**, I flip F6 and G5. This has a huge impact: I deny the opponent the option to play at D8 and flip E7. Other moves, like **C5**, make the situation worse by opening up those D7-D6 flips.\n\nHere\'s why **H4** is the best play: I want to prevent the opponent from getting edge pieces. If they play D8, I get only 1 piece. **H4** flips just 1 piece. So by breaking the line, D8 won\'t impact as many of my existing tiles.\n\nSo, although an edge, by taking it I deny the opponent an edge-piece advantage. Also, by playing H4, I flip to my pieces on the board and can use them strategically.\n\n**Decision**\n\nThe move that strategically balances the above factors is, without a doubt, **H4**. This is the key.\n\n**Evaluation:** H4 is an edge move, and it\'s also a defensive play to block a potential opponent.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: H4 is an excellent move that secures a stable edge position while serving a critical defensive purpose.\nReason: Currently, the opponent has a strong potential move at D8, which would capture your piece at E7 (diagonally) and D7 (horizontally). Playing H4 flips F6 to your color, breaking the diagonal sandwich (D8-E7-F6). This reduces the damage of an opponent's D8 response from capturing multiple key pieces to just one. Other options like C5 are dangerous as they would allow D8 to capture three pieces (D6, D7, and E7). H4 balances edge stability with threat mitigation.\n\nAnswer: (H, 4)"}
